<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Joaquín Pérez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Joaquín Pérez was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> from 1861 to 1871, the last of the four presidents who each served two consecutive five-year terms. A moderate conservative, he governed through broad coalitions and eased the conflicts left by the Montt era. His presidency saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands-war">Chincha Islands War</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and advances into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> lands in Araucanía.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-joaquin-perez.jpg" alt="José Joaquín Pérez" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Joaqu%C3%ADn_Perez.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Joaquín Pérez Mascayano was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> on 6 May 1801, the son of the lawyer and politician Santiago Pérez Salas and María de la Luz Mascayano y Larraín. He studied humanities at the Real Colegio Carolino and the Instituto Nacional and took a law degree at the Royal University of San Felipe. His early career was in diplomacy: secretary of the Chilean legation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a> in 1826, chargé d'affaires in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1829 and representative in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1830. On 9 April 1840 he married Tránsito Florez de la Cavareda; of their eleven children five daughters survived to adulthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pérez entered politics in 1826 as a deputy for Aconcagua Province and later held seats for Puchacay, Curicó and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. He served as Minister of the Interior and Minister of Foreign Relations under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-bulnes">Manuel Bulnes</a>. As a senator and state councillor under Manuel Montt he stayed neutral in political conflicts, which made him a unifying candidate for the presidency in 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As president Pérez promoted reconciliation and reform. He enacted an amnesty for political disputes of 1851 to 1861, allowed greater freedom of the press and passed the Law of Freedom of Worship in 1865. His government completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> railway and, after the fire at the Church of the Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in 1863, established the Santiago Fire Department.</p><p>He signed the 1866 border treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and conducted a national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, which recorded a population of over 1.8 million. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> expanded into Araucanía during his presidency, fighting the indigenous inhabitants. Re-elected in 1866, he faced debts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands-war">Chincha Islands War</a> (1865–1866) against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Shortly before he left office in 1871 a constitutional reform prohibited immediate re-election. He later served as a senator and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> in 1889, aged 88.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Joaquín Pérez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Four presidents, from Joaquín Prieto to <b>José Joaquín Pérez</b>, each served two consecutive five-year terms between 1831 and 1871, giving Chile a stability rare in nineteenth-century Spanish America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.44,-70.65&amp;year=1858" aria-label="Santiago on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of José Joaquín Pérez"><image href="/maps/globes/g300s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4244 5329h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4244 5329h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.44,-70.65&amp;year=1858">What was here in 1858? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
